Summary fitting two similar SEMs on the same dataset with posterior
predictive checks enabled in order to calculate a Bayes factor with
`BF()`, one of them crashes every time and works only if the dataset
is standardized/scaled. Also, PPCs are slow partially because they're
serial.

I have been looking at the survey results in
http://slatestarcodex.com/2016/06/28/survey-results-suffering-vs-oblivion/
(raw data: http://slatestarcodex.com/blog_images/oblivionsurvey.csv ;
cleaned up: https://www.dropbox.com/s/xykunut6clb5f66/2016-06-30-oblivionsurvey-clean.csv
) about population ethics and checking the author's single-factor
interpretation using SEMs rather than correlations.

Based on some exploratory factor analysis and clusterings, I wrote
down a second model which has two factors instead and which I thought
made sense of the clusters, to compare the chi-squared fit in lavaan
and also get a Bayes factor from blavaan.
The first model fit in blavaan without any problem, and the posterior
predictive checks didn't take as long as I feared they might (although
I noticed R was using only one core during the PPCs, and checking the
`post_pred.R` code, it looks like it's a serial loop even though PPC
should be an embarrassingly parallel algorithm? that probably doesn't
help). The second model blew up repeatedly when I tried:

